    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This study provides the first geological evidence for state-dependent climate sensitivity by combining a new high-resolution Eocene CO\u2082 record with global temperature estimates, demonstrating that sensitivity was higher during warmer climates and declined as the Earth cooled.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "cGENIE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "calcite saturation in surface waters",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Reconstructions of atmospheric CO2. The derived surface seawater pH estimates from foraminiferal 811B were combined with the latitude-specific estimates of calcite saturation in surface waters (from cGENIE4), which we assume remains within a range of \u00b11, thereby accounting for uncertainty in both absolute value and any short-term variability16. Full error propagation was carried out using a Monte Carlo approach as described in ref. 4.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Pacific deep-water formation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Large scale circulation changes could cause this 813C-CO2 decoupling, but they preceded the EECO by ca. 6 My41, except the short-lived changes in deep water formation during hyperthermal events, such as the PETM42. Additionally, cessation of North Pacific deep-water formation43, a more inter-basin thermohaline circulation 813C pattern44 (Fig. 3f), and establishment of a proto-Antarctic Circumpolar circulation (proto-ACC) associated with the gradual Drake Passage opening (ref. 45) and the Tasman Seaway widening (refs. 46,47) followed the EECO CO2 and temperature decline (post 47 Ma). Therefore circulation changes are unlikely to have been the main drivers of the 813C and CO2 decoupling within the EECO.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "CAM 41",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "GMT calculations. We convert the ODP 959 TEX86 SST record of ref. 1 to GMT, employing previously published model simulations with the NCAR CESM version 1 with CAM 41, which essentially provides a transfer function from SST at ODP 959 to a global mean in four specific time windows (54-49, 48-46, 42-42, 38-35 Ma; Supplementary Fig. 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Global Mean Temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Global mean temperature and climate sensitivity. Regardless of the causes of the evolution of CO2 through the early Cenozoic, our new CO2 record clearly resembles long-term deep-sea and surface seawater temperature (SST) records as compiled in refs. 1,2,38-40,55, (see \"Methods\", Fig. 4). To further explore the relationship between CO2 and the global mean temperature evolution during the Eocene, we first computed GMT (Methods). However here, rather than using multi-site, non-continuous foraminiferal 818O records, which have also been shown to be impacted by diagenesis56,57, we use the continuous TEX86-SST record from the equatorial Atlantic (ODP 959)1 and the model simulations with the NCAR Community Earth System Model version 1 (CESM 1) in ref. 1, which provide a transfer function from SST at ODP 959 to a global mean in four specific time windows (54-49, 48-46, 42-42, 38\u201335 Ma; Supplementary Fig. 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
